# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
# Unanchored and applied at any depth, because `client/node_modules` only ever covered the client:
# docker/mcp-server/node_modules was a second install of 3,766 files that nothing was ignoring, and
# every future package under docker/ would have had the same problem one directory at a time.
node_modules/
/.pnp
.pnp.js

# testing
/coverage

# production
# `/build` is anchored to the repository root, so it never matched the directory that actually
# exists: client/build. Thirteen files were tracked there as a result. Every one of them is a copy
# of something in client/public, and the image rebuilds the directory from source (client/Dockerfile
# runs `npm run build`), so nothing here is worth versioning.
/build
client/build/

# Runtime state, not source.
#
# wordlists/ffuf is the FFUF upload directory (ffufUtils.go:163). Whatever an operator uploads for
# one engagement lands here under a UUID prefix, which is per-machine data that means nothing in
# anyone else's checkout. The directory is created on demand by os.MkdirAll, so ignoring it whole
# cannot break a fresh clone. The wordlists that SHIP with the framework live one level up in
# wordlists/ and stay tracked: ffuf-wordlist-5000.txt, ffuf-headers.txt, param-names.txt and the
# rest are referenced by name from the Go code and the app breaks without them.
wordlists/ffuf/
wordlists/sniper_test.txt

# Scratch files from live testing. These are captured request bodies, probe configs and one-off
# helper scripts written while working a target; they are meaningful for an afternoon and confusing
# forever after.
/run*.json
/repro.txt
/probe_post.py
/server/run_live.json

# misc
.DS_Store
.env.local
.env.development.local
.env.test.local
.env.production.local

npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

MANUAL_GUIDE.md